/* 自动生成，请勿手工编辑。生成脚本：tools/build_data.py */
window.CET4_PASSAGES={"meta":{"count":10},"passages":[{"id":"r01","title":"The Cost of Being Always Online","topic":"科技与互联网","level":2,"difficulty":"中等","text":"For most of human history, being unreachable was normal. A letter might take weeks to arrive, and nobody expected an answer the same day. Today a message that goes unanswered for two hours can feel like an insult. Smartphones have not simply added a new tool to our lives; they have changed what we expect from one another. Researchers who study communication describe this shift as the rise of an always-on culture, a set of habits built on the assumption that everyone can be reached at any moment. The assumption is convenient, but it is not free.\n\nThe first cost is attention. Studies of office workers suggest that after an interruption a person needs more than twenty minutes to return to the same level of concentration. A short reply to a message therefore costs far more than the thirty seconds it appears to take. Some companies have responded by creating quiet hours, periods when internal messages are not delivered at all. Early reports from these firms are positive: workers say they finish difficult tasks faster and feel less tired at the end of the day.\n\nThe second cost is harder to measure. When people are never fully alone, they lose the slow, unplanned thinking that boredom used to produce. A walk without a phone, a wait at a bus stop, a long shower: these were once the places where new ideas appeared. Psychologists note that our best thinking often happens when the mind is not directed at a task. Filling every spare minute with a screen removes exactly those moments.\n\nNone of this means that smartphones are a disaster. They connect families across continents and give students access to more information than any library contains. The question is not whether to use them but how much of our attention to hand over. Some people now choose to leave the phone in another room while they work, or to keep notifications off by default. These small decisions are not a rejection of technology. They are an attempt to decide, deliberately, when we want to be reachable and when we do not.","translation":"在人类历史的大部分时间里，联系不上别人是常态。一封信可能要几周才能送到，没人指望当天就得到回复。今天，一条消息两个小时没有回复就可能让人觉得受到冒犯。智能手机不只是给我们的生活增添了一件新工具，它们还改变了我们对彼此的期待。研究传播的学者把这种变化称为随时在线文化的兴起——一种建立在任何人都能随时被联系到这一假定之上的习惯。这个假定很方便，但它并非没有代价。\n\n第一个代价是注意力。针对办公室职员的研究表明，被打断之后，一个人需要二十多分钟才能恢复到原先的专注程度。因此，回复一条消息看似只需三十秒，实际代价却远不止三十秒。一些公司为此设立了安静时段，在这段时间里内部消息根本不会送达。这些公司早期的反馈是正面的：员工说他们能更快完成困难的任务，一天下来也没那么疲惫。\n\n第二个代价更难衡量。当人从不完全独处时，就失去了无聊曾经带来的那种缓慢、无计划的思考。一次不带手机的散步、在公交站的等待、一次长时间的淋浴——这些曾经都是新想法出现的地方。心理学家指出，我们最好的思考往往发生在头脑不专注于某项任务的时候。用屏幕填满每一分钟空闲，恰恰抹掉了这些时刻。\n\n这一切并不意味着智能手机是一场灾难。它们让相隔大洲的家庭保持联系，让学生能够获取比任何图书馆都多的信息。问题不在于是否使用它们，而在于我们要交出多少注意力。如今有些人选择工作时把手机放在另一个房间，或者默认关闭通知提醒。这些小的决定并不是对技术的拒绝，而是试图有意识地决定：我们想什么时候被联系到，什么时候不想。","questions":[{"id":"r01q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The reasons why smartphones have become cheaper and more powerful.","The hidden costs of an always-on culture and the way individuals can respond to it.","The history of letter writing before the invention of the telephone.","The methods that companies use to monitor their employees at work."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。全文结构：第1段末句点出随时在线文化这一假定并非没有代价，第2段讲注意力代价，第3段讲无聊消失后思考受损，第4段提出个人可以刻意决定何时可被联系。A、C文中未提；D只是第2段末尾提到的 quiet hours 这一细节，不能概括全文。"},{"id":"r01q2","stem":"According to paragraph 2, why does a quick reply to a message cost more than it appears to?","options":["Because it uses a large amount of mobile data.","Because it may break a company rule about quiet hours.","Because the person needs over twenty minutes to regain full concentration afterwards.","Because the receiver usually answers with another question."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在第2段第2句：after an interruption a person needs more than twenty minutes to return to the same level of concentration，随后一句说回复消息的代价远不止三十秒。A、D文中未提；B偷换了范围，quiet hours 是公司采取的对策，不是回复代价的原因。"},{"id":"r01q3","stem":"What did the early reports from companies with quiet hours show?","options":["Workers sent fewer messages to customers.","Workers asked for shorter working days.","The companies saved money on office equipment.","Workers finished difficult tasks faster and felt less tired."],"answer":3,"explain":"答案D。依据在第2段最后一句：workers say they finish difficult tasks faster and feel less tired at the end of the day。A、B、C均属绝对化或无中生有，原文只说内部消息在安静时段不送达，并未提客户消息、工时要求或设备开支。"},{"id":"r01q4","stem":"What can be inferred about boredom from paragraph 3?","options":["It should be avoided because it lowers efficiency in the office.","It is a state that modern devices are removing, together with some valuable thinking.","It is the main cause of unhappiness among office workers.","It appears only when people are walking or waiting for a bus."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。第3段说人若从不完全独处，就会失去无聊带来的缓慢、无计划的思考，而这些时刻正是新想法出现之处；末句又说用屏幕填满每一分钟空闲恰恰抹掉了这些时刻，故可推知B。A与原文相反；C文中未提；D把举例当成了唯一条件，属偷换范围。"},{"id":"r01q5","stem":"The phrase hand over in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to","options":["repair","hide","give up control of","record"],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。末段第3句 The question is not whether to use them but how much of our attention to hand over，与后文 leave the phone in another room、keep notifications off 形成呼应，指交出注意力的控制权。A、B、D均与上下文语义不符。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"assume","zh":"假定，认为"},{"w":"interruption","zh":"打断，干扰"},{"w":"concentration","zh":"专注，注意力集中"},{"w":"deliberately","zh":"刻意地，有意地"},{"w":"notification","zh":"通知，消息提示"},{"w":"spare","zh":"空闲的，多余的"},{"w":"measure","zh":"衡量，测量"},{"w":"access","zh":"获取，使用的机会"}]},{"id":"r02","title":"Cities Learning to Live with Extreme Heat","topic":"环境与气候","level":3,"difficulty":"较难","text":"In 2023 more than fifty cities around the world recorded their highest temperatures ever measured. For city planners the problem is no longer whether heat waves will come but how often. Concrete and asphalt absorb sunlight during the day and release it slowly at night, so a large city can be several degrees warmer than the farmland that once surrounded it. Scientists call this the urban heat island effect. It is one reason why a hot night in a city can be more dangerous than a hot afternoon in the countryside.\n\nThe most direct solution is also the oldest: trees. A single mature tree can cool the air around it as much as several small air conditioners, and it does so without using electricity. Yet planting trees is slower and more complicated than it appears. A young tree may need twenty years and regular watering before it provides real shade. Space is scarce, pipes run under many streets, and trees need care that poor neighbourhoods often cannot afford. As a result, the shade in many cities follows the money.\n\nSome cities have turned to paint and design instead. Painting roofs white reflects sunlight and can lower indoor temperatures by several degrees at a cost far below that of air conditioning. In Los Angeles, workers have covered streets with a light grey coating that reduces surface heat. Rethinking the timetable is another approach: Athens now closes some public spaces during the hottest hours and keeps them open late into the evening, and schools move their sports lessons to the early morning.\n\nNone of these measures replaces the need to reduce the gases that warm the planet. Critics warn that focusing on shade and white paint may make cities feel that they have solved a problem they have only softened. Supporters answer that both tasks must happen at once. Adaptation protects people today; cutting emissions protects everyone tomorrow. A city that waits for a global agreement before planting its first tree has simply chosen to lose twenty years of shade.","translation":"2023年，全球有五十多座城市记录下了有史以来的最高气温。对城市规划者而言，问题已不再是热浪会不会来，而是多久来一次。混凝土和沥青白天吸收阳光，夜里缓慢释放热量，因此一座大城市可能比曾经环绕它的农田高出好几度。科学家称之为城市热岛效应。这也是城市里炎热的夜晚可能比乡下的炎热午后更危险的原因之一。\n\n最直接的解决办法也是最古老的：种树。一棵成年大树降温的效果相当于好几台小型空调，而且不用电。然而，种树比看上去要慢得多、也复杂得多。一棵幼树可能需要二十年时间和定期浇水，才能真正提供阴凉。空间稀缺，许多街道下面埋着管道，而树木所需的养护往往是贫困社区负担不起的。结果，在许多城市，阴凉是跟着钱走的。\n\n一些城市转而借助涂料和设计。把屋顶刷成白色可以反射阳光，能让室内温度下降好几度，成本却远低于空调。在洛杉矶，工人们给街道涂上浅灰色涂层以降低地表温度。重新安排时间表是另一种办法：雅典如今在最热的时段关闭一些公共场所，并让它们开放到深夜，学校则把体育课挪到清晨。\n\n这些措施都不能取代减少使地球变暖的气体。批评者警告说，只关注阴凉和白漆，可能让城市以为自己已经解决了一个其实仅仅被缓解了的问题。支持者则回答说，这两件事必须同时进行：适应措施保护今天的人，减排保护明天的所有人。一座等到全球协议签署后才种下第一棵树的城市，只是选择白白失去二十年的阴凉。","questions":[{"id":"r02q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The competition among cities to record the highest temperatures.","The measures cities are taking to cope with extreme heat and the argument about their limits.","The reasons why farmers are leaving the land around large cities.","The way air conditioners have changed the design of modern buildings."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。第1段提出城市高温问题，第2、3段分别介绍种树、刷白屋顶和调整时间表等措施，第4段讨论这些措施与减排之间的争论，故B最全面。A、C、D均为无中生有或把个别细节放大。"},{"id":"r02q2","stem":"According to paragraph 1, why can a hot night in a city be more dangerous than a hot afternoon in the countryside?","options":["Because city buildings release the heat they stored during the day.","Because hospitals in cities are usually short of staff at night.","Because city residents sleep with their windows closed.","Because the countryside is always cooler in summer."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第1段第3句：混凝土和沥青白天吸收阳光、夜里缓慢释放，因此城市比周围农田高出好几度，这就是热岛效应；末句据此指出城市的热夜更危险。B、C文中未提；D绝对化，乡村并非总是更凉爽。"},{"id":"r02q3","stem":"According to paragraph 2, what is one reason why planting trees in cities is difficult?","options":["A young tree may need about twenty years and regular watering before it provides real shade.","Trees cannot grow in the air of a modern city.","City residents often refuse to have trees in their streets.","Trees make air conditioning more expensive to run."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第2段第3句：A young tree may need twenty years and regular watering before it provides real shade，后面还补充了空间稀缺、地下有管道、贫困社区负担不起养护。B、C、D均属无中生有。"},{"id":"r02q4","stem":"The sentence the shade in many cities follows the money suggests that","options":["trees cost more to buy than air conditioners.","city governments spend their money on shade rather than other services.","poor residents prefer to live in sunny areas.","wealthier neighbourhoods are more likely to enjoy the cooling effect of trees."],"answer":3,"explain":"答案D。该句位于第2段末尾，前一句说树木需要贫困社区常常负担不起的养护，因此阴凉跟着钱走即指富裕地区更容易得到树木带来的降温。A偷换比较对象；B与原文因果不符；C无中生有。"},{"id":"r02q5","stem":"What is the author's attitude towards measures such as planting trees and painting roofs white?","options":["A firm belief that they are enough to solve the problem.","A recognition that they help people today but cannot replace cutting emissions.","Complete opposition to spending money on them.","Indifference to whether cities adopt them or not."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。末段第1句明确说这些措施不能取代减排，随后引用双方观点并以适应保护今天、减排保护明天作结，说明作者认可其现实作用但强调其局限。A绝对化；C、D与末段支持者所持立场相悖。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"absorb","zh":"吸收"},{"w":"release","zh":"释放，排放"},{"w":"mature","zh":"成熟的，长成的"},{"w":"shade","zh":"阴凉处，遮阴"},{"w":"scarce","zh":"稀少的，匮乏的"},{"w":"reflect","zh":"反射，反映"},{"w":"coating","zh":"涂层，覆盖层"},{"w":"adaptation","zh":"适应，适应措施"}]},{"id":"r03","title":"Should Students Still Learn Handwriting?","topic":"教育与校园","level":2,"difficulty":"中等","text":"In many schools the time given to handwriting has fallen sharply. Keyboards are faster, examinations are increasingly digital, and teachers have more content to cover than ever before. Yet a growing body of research suggests that the act of forming letters by hand does something for the brain that typing does not. The debate, therefore, is not about nostalgia for neat notebooks. It is about what kind of learning we want to encourage.\n\nThe strongest evidence comes from studies of note-taking. Students who write notes by hand usually record fewer words than those who type, because they cannot keep up with a speaker word for word. That limitation turns out to be an advantage. Unable to copy everything, hand-writers must decide what matters and summarise it in their own words. When both groups were tested later, the hand-writers remembered more, even though their notes were shorter.\n\nHandwriting may also help beginners learn to read. When children form a letter, they must notice its shape, its direction and the way it is built from parts. Brain scans show that this process activates areas involved in reading. Typing the same letter, by contrast, requires only a single press on a keyboard, and the shape of the letter never enters the child's mind. For young learners this difference can be important.\n\nFew researchers argue that schools should abandon typing. Digital skills are essential, and students with disabilities may depend on keyboards. A more reasonable position is that handwriting and typing serve different purposes and that both deserve a place in the timetable. The mistake would be to treat handwriting as a decorative skill that can be dropped whenever the curriculum becomes crowded, and then to discover, years later, what was lost.","translation":"在许多学校里，用于书写训练的时间大幅减少。键盘更快，考试越来越多地在电脑上进行，而教师要讲的内容比以往任何时候都多。然而，越来越多的研究显示，用手写字母这一动作对大脑有某种打字所没有的作用。因此，这场争论并不是对整齐笔记本的怀旧，而是关于我们想鼓励什么样的学习。\n\n最有力的证据来自关于记笔记的研究。用手记笔记的学生通常比打字的学生记得少，因为他们无法逐字跟上讲话者。这一局限结果却成了优势。由于无法抄下一切，手写者必须判断什么重要，并用自己的话把它概括出来。后来对两组进行测试时，手写者记住的内容更多，尽管他们的笔记更短。\n\n书写还可能帮助初学者学会阅读。当孩子书写一个字母时，他必须注意它的形状、方向以及它由哪些部分构成。脑部扫描显示，这一过程会激活与阅读有关的区域。相比之下，打出同一个字母只需在键盘上按一下，字母的形状从未进入孩子的头脑。对年幼的学习者来说，这种差别可能很重要。\n\n很少有研究者主张学校应当放弃打字。数字技能必不可少，而有残障的学生可能依赖键盘。更合理的立场是：书写和打字服务于不同的目的，两者都应在课程表中占有一席之地。真正的错误在于，把书写当作一种一旦课程变紧就可以砍掉的装饰性技能，然后在多年之后才发现，究竟失去了什么。","questions":[{"id":"r03q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why keyboards should replace handwriting in modern classrooms.","How to make children write neatly and quickly.","The evidence that handwriting helps learning and the reason it should be kept.","The history of writing tools from pen to keyboard."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。第1段提出争论焦点，第2段用记笔记实验说明手写的优势，第3段说明手写对初学阅读的帮助，第4段主张两者并存，故C概括最完整。A与末段主张相反；B、D文中未展开。"},{"id":"r03q2","stem":"According to paragraph 2, why did the hand-writers remember more?","options":["Because they had to decide what mattered and express it in their own words.","Because their notes were longer and more complete.","Because they heard every single word the speaker said.","Because they were tested immediately after the lecture."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第2段第3、4句：无法抄下全部内容，手写者必须判断什么重要并用自己的话概括，随后一句说他们记住的更多。B与原文相反，手写笔记更短；C与第2句 they cannot keep up word for word 矛盾；D属无中生有。"},{"id":"r03q3","stem":"What do brain scans show about children who form letters by hand?","options":["The activity has no measurable effect on the brain.","It makes children slower at learning to type.","It activates areas of the brain that are involved in reading.","It depends mainly on the age at which children start school."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在第3段第3句：Brain scans show that this process activates areas involved in reading。A与原文相反；B、D文中均未提及。"},{"id":"r03q4","stem":"What does the author suggest in the last paragraph?","options":["Schools should stop teaching typing altogether.","Handwriting and typing serve different purposes, so both should be kept.","Students with disabilities do not need handwriting lessons.","Handwriting should be taught only as an art lesson."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。依据在末段第3句：handwriting and typing serve different purposes and both deserve a place in the timetable。A与末段第1句相反；C把有残障学生可能依赖键盘这一细节当作结论；D无中生有。"},{"id":"r03q5","stem":"The word decorative in the last paragraph most probably means","options":["attractive but not really useful","extremely expensive","difficult for children to learn","traditional and old-fashioned"],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。该词所在句说把书写当作一种一旦课程变紧就可以丢弃的技能是错误，可见作者用它指只有装饰作用而无实际教育价值的技能，与aesthetic、ornamental 同义。B、C、D均非该词本义，也与上下文无关。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"handwriting","zh":"手写，书写"},{"w":"limitation","zh":"局限，限制"},{"w":"summarise","zh":"概括，总结"},{"w":"activate","zh":"激活，使活跃"},{"w":"essential","zh":"必不可少的"},{"w":"abandon","zh":"放弃，抛弃"},{"w":"decorative","zh":"装饰性的"},{"w":"curriculum","zh":"课程，课程设置"}]},{"id":"r04","title":"In Praise of the Walking Meeting","topic":"健康与运动","level":1,"difficulty":"简单","text":"Office workers in many countries spend most of the working day seated. One common estimate is that a typical employee sits for eight to ten hours, including the journey to and from work. Since the 1950s researchers have linked long periods of sitting with a higher risk of heart disease and diabetes. What is less widely known is that an hour at the gym does not fully cancel the effects of ten hours in a chair. The position of the body for most of the day matters on its own.\n\nOne simple response is the walking meeting. Instead of gathering around a table, two or three colleagues take a short walk together. Supporters point out that the meeting costs no extra time, since the participants would have been sitting anyway. A study of office employees found that those who walked during meetings reported more energy and a greater willingness to speak honestly. Some managers also report that walking side by side feels less like an examination than sitting face to face across a desk.\n\nThe idea does have limits. Detailed documents are hard to read while moving, and a group of more than four people becomes difficult to manage on a pavement. Not every conversation suits a walk. Sensitive discussions about pay or performance are usually better held indoors, where nobody can be overheard by strangers. In cold or rainy weather the plan may simply fail.\n\nUsed sensibly, however, walking meetings offer something that most health advice cannot: they require no equipment, no membership and no extra hour in the day. The main obstacle is habit rather than cost. Many organisations still assume that serious work happens only when everyone is seated and still. Changing that assumption is a small step, but for people who sit for a living, small steps repeated often are exactly what is needed.","translation":"在许多国家，办公室职员一天中的大部分时间都坐着。一个常见的估算是，一名普通员工每天要坐八到十个小时，其中包括上下班路上的时间。自20世纪50年代以来，研究者就把长时间久坐与更高的心脏病和糖尿病风险联系在一起。而不太为人所知的是，在健身房锻炼一小时并不能完全抵消在椅子上坐十个小时的影响。身体在一天中大部分时间里所处的姿态本身就有影响。\n\n一个简单的应对办法是边走边开会。两位或三位同事不再围着桌子坐，而是一起走一小段路。支持者指出，这种会议不额外占用时间，因为参加者本来也是坐着。一项针对办公室职员的研究发现，开会时走路的人报告说精力更充沛，也更愿意坦诚发言。一些管理者还说，并肩走路不像隔着桌子面对面坐着那样让人感觉像在被考查。\n\n这个想法确实有局限。边走边看详细文件很困难，而四个人以上的团队在人行道上难以兼顾。并非所有谈话都适合边走边说。涉及薪酬或绩效这类敏感话题，通常更适合在室内进行，那里不会有陌生人听到。遇上寒冷或下雨的天气，这个计划可能根本行不通。\n\n然而，只要使用得当，走路开会能提供大多数健康建议给不了的东西：它不需要器材，不需要会员卡，也不额外占用一天中的时间。主要障碍是习惯而不是成本。许多机构仍然认为，只有大家都坐着不动时才算在认真工作。改变这一观念只是一小步，但对靠久坐谋生的人来说，经常重复的小步恰恰正是所需要的。","questions":[{"id":"r04q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why exercise at the gym is the best protection against heart disease.","The benefits and the limitations of holding meetings while walking.","How modern offices are designed to encourage conversation.","The reasons why employees dislike face-to-face meetings."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。第1段说明久坐的危害，第2段介绍走路开会的优点，第3段列举其局限，第4段总结其优势与主要障碍，因此B最全面。A与第1段末句相反，文中说健身房一小时不能完全抵消久坐；C、D文中未提。"},{"id":"r04q2","stem":"According to paragraph 1, what is less widely known?","options":["Sitting for a long time is linked to heart disease.","A typical employee sits for eight to ten hours a day.","An hour at the gym does not fully cancel the effects of long sitting.","The journey to and from work is part of the working day."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在第1段第4句：What is less widely known is that an hour at the gym does not fully cancel the effects of ten hours in a chair。A、B是第1、2句已广为人知的内容；D是对 including the journey to and from work 的曲解。"},{"id":"r04q3","stem":"According to paragraph 2, what did a study of office employees find?","options":["Those who walked during meetings reported more energy and greater willingness to speak honestly.","Those who walked took much longer to reach a decision.","Those who walked preferred to meet in smaller groups.","Those who walked missed fewer days of work because of illness."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第2段第4句：a study of office employees found that those who walked during meetings reported more energy and a greater willingness to speak honestly。B、C、D均属无中生有，其中C把第3段关于人数限制的内容提前混入。"},{"id":"r04q4","stem":"What does the author suggest about sensitive discussions?","options":["They are impossible to hold in an office.","They should always be held outdoors in fine weather.","They are better held indoors so that strangers cannot overhear them.","They usually take more time during a walk."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在第3段第4句：Sensitive discussions about pay or performance are usually better held indoors, where nobody can be overheard by strangers。A、B绝对化；D文中未提。"},{"id":"r04q5","stem":"What is the author's attitude towards walking meetings?","options":["Completely opposed to them.","Supportive, while aware of their practical limits.","Neutral, offering no opinion of his own.","Worried mainly about their cost."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。第2段列举其优点，第3段承认局限，第4段用 Used sensibly, however 转折后肯定其价值，并指出主要障碍是习惯而非成本，可见态度是有限度地支持。A与全文相反；C与第4段明确评价不符；D与末段 The main obstacle is habit rather than cost 矛盾。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"estimate","zh":"估计，估算"},{"w":"link","zh":"把……联系起来"},{"w":"cancel","zh":"抵消，取消"},{"w":"colleague","zh":"同事"},{"w":"willingness","zh":"意愿，愿意"},{"w":"sensitive","zh":"敏感的"},{"w":"obstacle","zh":"障碍，阻碍"},{"w":"equipment","zh":"设备，器材"}]},{"id":"r05","title":"The Four-Day Week Experiment","topic":"职场与经济","level":2,"difficulty":"中等","text":"In 2022 dozens of companies in Britain took part in a six-month trial of a four-day working week. Employees received the same pay and worked one day less. In most of the participating firms output did not fall. Managers reported that meetings became shorter, that unnecessary reports disappeared, and that staff arrived with more energy on Monday. At the end of the trial most companies decided to keep the new arrangement.\n\nThe results are striking, but they should be read with care. The firms that joined the experiment were mostly small, and many worked in offices where output is measured in projects rather than in hours. A software company can sometimes finish a task in four days by removing distractions. A hospital, a factory or a restaurant cannot simply close on Fridays. The trial therefore tells us a good deal about knowledge work and rather little about jobs where someone must be present at a fixed time.\n\nThere is also a question of scale. In a small firm, saving time is easy to notice and easy to reward. In a large organisation the same worker must coordinate with many colleagues, and a day when half the team is absent can slow everyone down. Some economists argue that the real gain of a shorter week is not fewer hours but better attention: when time is limited, people protect it more carefully.\n\nWhatever the final verdict, the experiment has changed the terms of the argument. For a century the debate about working time was mostly about money, that is, whether workers could afford to work less. That question has not disappeared, but a second one now sits beside it: whether organisations can organise themselves well enough to need fewer hours. The trial suggests that in some workplaces the answer is yes, and that the main obstacle may be imagination rather than economics.","translation":"2022年，英国数十家公司参加了一项为期六个月的每周四天工作制试验。员工薪水不变，但每周少上一天班。在大多数参与公司中，产出并没有下降。管理者报告说会议变短了，不必要的报告消失了，员工周一上班时精力更充沛。试验结束时，大多数公司决定保留这一新安排。\n\n这些结果令人瞩目，但解读时应保持谨慎。参与试验的公司大多规模较小，而且许多是产出以项目而非工时来衡量的办公室工作。一家软件公司有时可以通过排除干扰在四天内完成任务，而医院、工厂或餐馆不可能星期五就关门。因此，这项试验在很大程度上说明了知识型工作的情况，而对那些必须有人在固定时间到岗的工作却说明得很少。\n\n还有规模的问题。在小公司里，节省时间容易察觉，也容易给予奖励。而在大型机构中，同一个员工必须与许多同事协调，如果某天团队里有一半人不在，就可能拖慢所有人。一些经济学家认为，缩短工作周真正的收获并不是工时更少，而是注意力更集中：当时间有限时，人们会更小心地保护它。\n\n无论最终结论如何，这项试验已经改变了争论的前提。一个世纪以来，关于工作时间的争论主要围绕钱，也就是工人是否有能力少工作。这个问题并没有消失，但如今它旁边又出现了第二个问题：机构能否把自己组织得足够好，从而需要更少的工时。这项试验表明，在某些工作场所答案是肯定的，而主要障碍也许不是经济条件，而是想象力。","questions":[{"id":"r05q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why British companies have decided to close on Fridays.","The results of a four-day week trial and the limits of what it proves.","How hospitals and factories can shorten their working hours.","The history of the working week since the nineteenth century."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。第1段介绍试验结果，第2段指出试验对象多为小型办公室公司因而结论有限，第3段谈规模问题，第4段总结争论焦点的变化，均围绕试验结果及其适用限度。A、C与第2段明确说明的医院、工厂不能关门矛盾；D文中未展开。"},{"id":"r05q2","stem":"Which of the following is true of the trial described in paragraph 1?","options":["Employees were paid less for four days of work.","Output fell slightly in most of the companies.","The trial lasted for two years.","Employees kept the same pay and worked one day less, and output generally did not fall."],"answer":3,"explain":"答案D。依据在第1段第2、3句：Employees received the same pay and worked one day less. In most of the participating firms output did not fall。A与 same pay 矛盾；B与原文相反；C与 six-month trial 不符。"},{"id":"r05q3","stem":"According to paragraph 2, why should the results be read with care?","options":["Because the trial was too short to show anything at all.","Because the firms were mostly small and did office work measured by projects rather than hours.","Because employees were not honest in the surveys.","Because managers were paid to report positive results."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。依据在第2段第2句：参与试验的公司多为小型企业，许多是产出以项目而非工时衡量的办公室工作，随后以软件公司对比医院、工厂和餐馆。A绝对化；C、D属无中生有。"},{"id":"r05q4","stem":"What can be inferred from paragraph 3 about large organisations?","options":["A shorter week is harder for them because workers depend on many colleagues.","They have already adopted the four-day week.","They measure output more accurately than small firms do.","They pay higher wages than small firms do."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第3段第2句：大机构中员工必须与许多同事协调，若某天团队一半人不在就会拖慢所有人，故可推知缩短工作周在大机构更难。B与第2段说明的适用对象不符；C、D文中未提。"},{"id":"r05q5","stem":"The word verdict in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to","options":["a decision or judgement","a punishment","a payment","a complaint"],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。Whatever the final verdict 意为无论最终结论如何，与后文 the experiment has changed the terms of the argument 相衔接，指对试验的最终判定。B、C、D均与语境不符。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"trial","zh":"试验，试行"},{"w":"output","zh":"产出，产量"},{"w":"participate","zh":"参加，参与"},{"w":"distraction","zh":"使人分心的事物"},{"w":"coordinate","zh":"协调，配合"},{"w":"scale","zh":"规模，范围"},{"w":"verdict","zh":"结论，判定"},{"w":"arrangement","zh":"安排，做法"}]},{"id":"r06","title":"Museums Without Walls","topic":"文化交流","level":2,"difficulty":"中等","text":"A student in a small town may never visit the Louvre, but she can now examine its paintings in greater detail than most visitors who stand in front of them. Major museums have photographed hundreds of thousands of objects and placed the images online free of charge. Some offer virtual tours that allow a viewer to move through a gallery room by room. What was once available only to those who could travel is now, in a limited sense, available to anyone with a connection.\n\nThe advantages go beyond convenience. An image online can be enlarged far beyond the size of the original, so a scholar in another country can study the brushwork of a painting or the marks on a coin. Small museums that own interesting collections but attract few visitors can reach audiences they could never serve in person. In this way digital access does not only spread culture; it also makes new research possible.\n\nYet the screen is not a neutral window. A camera decides where to look. Objects are lit for photography, arranged in a particular order, and often shown against a plain background that removes them from the messy world in which they were once used. Visitors who walk through a gallery see objects next to one another and can compare them by turning their heads. Online, they see only what the museum has chosen to show. The selection is invisible, and that is precisely where its power lies.\n\nMost museum directors now accept that the future is neither purely digital nor purely physical. The screen brings people in; the room keeps them. Surveys repeatedly show that online visitors who enjoy a collection are more likely to plan a real visit, not less. The task for museums is therefore to use the digital door without pretending that it is the same as standing in the hall, where a painting seen at its true size and in real light can still surprise someone who thought they already knew it.","translation":"一个小镇上的学生也许永远没机会参观卢浮宫，但如今她可以比大多数站在画前的参观者更仔细地观看那些画作。大型博物馆已经拍摄了数十万件藏品，并把这些图像免费放到网上。有些博物馆还提供虚拟导览，让观看者一个展厅一个展厅地走动。过去只有能够出行的人才能看到的东西，如今在有限的意义上，任何有网络连接的人都能看到。\n\n这些好处不止于方便。网上的图像可以放大到远超原件尺寸的程度，因此身处另一国的学者也能研究一幅画的笔触或一枚硬币上的痕迹。那些拥有有趣藏品却访客寥寥的小博物馆，也能触及它们在现场永远无法服务的受众。可见数字获取不仅传播了文化，也让新的研究成为可能。\n\n然而，屏幕并不是一扇中立的窗。是摄像机决定了看哪里。物品为了拍摄被打了光，被按特定顺序排列，还常常被置于纯色背景前，从而脱离了它们曾经被使用的那个杂乱世界。走进展厅的参观者能看到彼此相邻的物品，可以转过头去比较它们；而在网上，他们只看到博物馆选择展示的内容。这种挑选是无形的，而它的力量恰恰就在这里。\n\n如今大多数博物馆馆长都承认，未来既不是纯粹数字的，也不是纯粹实体的。屏幕把人吸引进来，而展厅把人留住。多项调查反复显示，喜欢某个线上收藏的观众更有可能计划一次真实的参观，而不是更少。因此，博物馆的任务是善用这扇数字之门，又不去假装它等同于站在展厅里——在那里，一幅以真实尺寸、在真实光线下看到的画，仍然能让自以为早已熟悉它的人感到惊讶。","questions":[{"id":"r06q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why the Louvre attracts more visitors than any other museum.","The cost of photographing objects in major museums.","How online access to museum collections helps people and how it differs from a real visit.","Why scholars prefer photographs to original objects."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。第1段讲网上观展的普及，第2段讲其好处（放大细节、小馆触达受众），第3段指出屏幕经过了挑选与安排，第4段总结数字与现实的关系，故C最全面。A、B属无中生有；D与第2段学者利用图像做研究而非取代原件不符。"},{"id":"r06q2","stem":"According to paragraph 2, how do online images help scholars?","options":["They can be enlarged far beyond the size of the original object.","They are sold at a lower price than printed books.","They are updated more often than museum labels.","They allow visitors to order copies of famous paintings."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第2段第2句：An image online can be enlarged far beyond the size of the original, so a scholar in another country can study the brushwork of a painting or the marks on a coin。B、C、D均属无中生有。"},{"id":"r06q3","stem":"What point does the author make in paragraph 3?","options":["Cameras are too expensive for small museums to buy.","Online visitors usually lose interest very quickly.","What viewers see online has already been selected and arranged by others.","Museums should stop photographing their objects."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在第3段第2至4句：摄像机决定看哪里，物品被打光、按顺序排列、置于纯色背景前，末句说这种挑选是无形的正是其力量所在。A、B、D均属无中生有。"},{"id":"r06q4","stem":"What do surveys repeatedly show about online visitors?","options":["They are much less likely to visit museums in person.","They are more likely to plan a real visit.","They spend less money in museum shops.","They prefer small museums to large ones."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。依据在第4段第3句：Surveys repeatedly show that online visitors who enjoy a collection are more likely to plan a real visit, not less。A与原文相反；C、D文中未提。"},{"id":"r06q5","stem":"The author's attitude towards digital museum collections is best described as","options":["hostile, seeing them as a threat to real museums.","favourable, while recognising that they cannot replace a real visit.","completely neutral, with no judgement offered.","worried that they will make research impossible."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。第2段肯定其便利与研究价值，第3段指出屏幕并非中立之窗，第4段明确说未来既非纯数字也非纯实体，屏幕把人吸引进来而展厅把人留住，故为有条件地肯定。A、D与文中肯定的部分矛盾；C忽略了作者的明确评价。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"collection","zh":"收藏品，藏品"},{"w":"virtual","zh":"虚拟的"},{"w":"gallery","zh":"画廊，展厅"},{"w":"scholar","zh":"学者"},{"w":"neutral","zh":"中立的"},{"w":"selection","zh":"挑选，所选之物"},{"w":"audience","zh":"观众，受众"},{"w":"physical","zh":"实体的，物质的"}]},{"id":"r07","title":"The Return of the Small Town","topic":"城市化","level":3,"difficulty":"较难","text":"For most of the twentieth century the story of the city was a story of growth. Young people left small towns for brighter streets and better jobs, and the towns they left grew older and quieter. In the past few years, however, that movement has slowed in several countries. Remote work has loosened the tie between where a person lives and where a person is employed, and some families have decided that space and quiet are worth more than a short journey to the office.\n\nThe economic effects are mixed. A new resident with a city salary can spend money in local shops and support a restaurant that was close to closing. House prices, however, rise quickly in a town with few homes for sale, and the people who work in those shops may find that they can no longer afford to live near them. The same arrival that saves a business can push a neighbour out. Small towns that welcome newcomers without building new housing often trade one problem for another.\n\nServices are harder to restore than populations. A town that loses its bank, its doctor and its secondary school does not get them back simply because more people move in. Each of these requires trained staff, and trained staff usually follow careers rather than scenery. Local governments have tried to attract them with housing support and better transport links, with limited success. A village can gain residents in a single year; it may take a decade to gain a hospital.\n\nThe return to small towns is therefore not a simple reversal of history. It depends on technology, on housing policy and on services that no individual family can provide. Whether it lasts will depend less on how many people move than on whether the places they move to can offer a full life rather than a pleasant view. Growth, in other words, is easy to start and difficult to keep. Towns that understand this are already planning for the second decade rather than the first.","translation":"在二十世纪的大部分时间里，城市的故事就是增长的故事。年轻人离开小城镇，去往更明亮的街道和更好的工作，而他们离开的城镇则变得更老、更安静。然而近年来，这种流动在一些国家已经放缓。远程工作松开了居住地与工作地之间的绑定，一些家庭认定，空间和安静比每天短途通勤去办公室更值得。\n\n经济上的影响是复杂的。一个拿着城市薪水的新居民会在本地商店消费，养活一家濒临倒闭的餐馆。然而，在待售房源很少的城镇，房价会迅速上涨，而那些在商店里工作的人可能发现自己再也负担不起住在附近。拯救了一家生意的同一批新来者，也可能把一位邻居挤出小镇。欢迎新居民却不建新房的小镇，往往是用一个问题换另一个问题。\n\n服务比人口更难恢复。一个失去了银行、医生和中学的城镇，不会仅仅因为搬来更多人就把它们重新拥有。这些服务都需要受过训练的人员，而受过训练的人通常是跟着职业前景走，而不是跟着风景走。地方政府试图用住房补贴和更好的交通来吸引他们，成效有限。一个村庄可能一年之内就增加居民，却可能要花十年才得到一家医院。\n\n因此，回到小城镇并不是历史简单的逆转。它取决于技术、住房政策，以及任何单个家庭都无法提供的服务。这种趋势能否持续，与其说取决于有多少人搬来，不如说取决于他们搬去的地方能否提供完整的生活，而不只是宜人的风景。换言之，增长容易开始，却难以维持。明白这一点的城镇，已经在为第二个十年而不是头几年做规划了。","questions":[{"id":"r07q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why young people continue to prefer life in large cities.","How remote work has brought down house prices in small towns.","The environmental benefits of moving away from big cities.","The recent movement of people to small towns and the difficulty of sustaining it."],"answer":3,"explain":"答案D。第1段讲人口流向的放缓，第2段讲经济影响的两面，第3段讲服务难以恢复，第4段总结这种回归不易持续，故D最全面。A与第1段所述趋势相反；B与第2段房价上涨的说法矛盾；C文中未提环境效益。"},{"id":"r07q2","stem":"According to paragraph 2, what problem can newcomers cause in a small town?","options":["They usually refuse to shop in local stores.","Rising house prices may force local workers to move away.","They take jobs away from long-time residents.","They demand better public transport from the town."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。依据在第2段第3、4句：房价在待售房很少的城镇迅速上涨，商店员工可能负担不起住在附近，The same arrival that saves a business can push a neighbour out。A与第2句 new resident can spend money in local shops 相反；C、D无中生有。"},{"id":"r07q3","stem":"According to paragraph 3, why is it hard for a small town to restore its services?","options":["Because local governments have no money at all.","Because residents do not want a hospital nearby.","Because services need trained staff, who tend to follow careers rather than pleasant surroundings.","Because small towns are too far from major roads."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在第3段第3句：Each of these requires trained staff, and trained staff usually follow careers rather than scenery，第4句则说地方政府用住房补贴和交通改善吸引人才但成效有限。A绝对化；B、D文中未提。"},{"id":"r07q4","stem":"What does the author imply by saying that growth is easy to start and difficult to keep?","options":["Population growth always slows down after a few years.","Attracting people to a town is easier than giving them a full life there.","Small towns should stop trying to attract new residents.","Economic growth is the only reliable measure of success."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。依据在末段第3句：能否持续取决于迁入地能否提供完整的生活而不只是宜人的风景，末句又说需要为第二个十年做规划。A绝对化；C与作者并未劝小城镇放弃发展不符；D无中生有。"},{"id":"r07q5","stem":"The word reversal in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to","options":["a turning back","a repetition","an improvement","an accident"],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。首句说回到小城镇并非历史简单的逆转，即不是对二十世纪人口外流趋势的简单反向，故reversal意为转向、逆转。B、C、D均与上下文语义不符。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"resident","zh":"居民，住户"},{"w":"salary","zh":"薪水，工资"},{"w":"restore","zh":"恢复，重建"},{"w":"afford","zh":"负担得起"},{"w":"decade","zh":"十年"},{"w":"reversal","zh":"逆转，反向变化"},{"w":"policy","zh":"政策，方针"},{"w":"scenery","zh":"风景，景色"}]},{"id":"r08","title":"Grandparents in the Childcare Economy","topic":"老年人/代际","level":2,"difficulty":"中等","text":"In many countries the childcare system quietly depends on people who are not paid for it. Grandparents collect children from school, cook meals, and fill the gap between the end of the school day and the end of the working day. Surveys in Europe suggest that grandparents provide a large share of all informal childcare. If this work had to be bought at market prices, many family budgets would collapse.\n\nThe arrangement suits everyone up to a point. Parents save money and worry less; children spend time with adults who love them; grandparents gain company and a sense of purpose. Research on older people repeatedly finds that those who care for grandchildren several days a week report better mental health than those who do not. The relationship is not merely a favour; it is a form of exchange that both sides value.\n\nThere are costs that appear later. Caring for young children is physically demanding, and grandparents who take on a full working week may neglect their own health. Some give up paid work earlier than they intended, which reduces their savings for old age. Others find that they cannot refuse, because the alternative for their children is a bill they cannot pay. Generosity offered freely is one thing; generosity that is expected is another.\n\nPolicy has been slow to notice. Public childcare is discussed as a service for parents, and pensions are discussed as a matter for older people, yet the two are joined by an invisible bridge. Some countries now give grandparents a small payment or count caring years towards a pension. Such measures are modest, but they recognise a simple fact: a society that relies on unpaid care should at least know what it is using, and who is paying for it.","translation":"在许多国家，儿童照护体系悄悄依赖着一些无偿付出的人。祖父母去学校接孩子、做饭，填补从放学到我下班之间的那段空档。欧洲的调查显示，祖父母提供了全部非正式托育中很大的一部分。如果这份工作必须按市场价格购买，许多家庭的预算就会崩溃。\n\n这种安排在某种程度上对各方都有利。父母省钱又省心；孩子与爱他们的成年人共度时光；祖父母获得陪伴和一种被需要的感觉。对老年人的研究反复发现，每周照看孙辈几天的人，其心理健康状况好于不照看的人。这种关系不只是帮忙，而是一种双方都看重的交换。\n\n也有一些代价是后来才显现的。照看幼儿在体力上很吃力，承担整周工作的祖父母可能忽视自己的健康。有些人比原计划更早放弃有薪工作，这减少了他们养老的储蓄。还有人发现自己无法拒绝，因为对子女来说，替代方案是一笔他们付不起的账单。自愿给予的慷慨是一回事，被视作理所当然的慷慨是另一回事。\n\n政策对此反应迟缓。公共托育被当作面向父母的服务来讨论，养老金被当作老年人的事务来讨论，可两者之间却由一座看不见的桥相连。一些国家如今给祖父母一小笔补贴，或把照护年限计入养老金。这类措施力度不大，但它们承认了一个简单的事实：一个依赖无偿照护的社会，至少应当知道自己在使用什么，又是谁在为此付出。","questions":[{"id":"r08q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why parents in Europe cannot afford professional childcare.","The health problems of older people who live alone.","The value and the hidden costs of childcare provided by grandparents.","How governments calculate the cost of raising a child."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。第1段指出祖父母承担了大量无偿托育，第2段讲其好处，第3段讲随之而来的代价，第4段讲政策层面的迟缓反应，故C最全面。A只是第1段末句的假设；B、D均无中生有。"},{"id":"r08q2","stem":"What does research on older people repeatedly find?","options":["Those who care for grandchildren several days a week report better mental health than those who do not.","Those who care for grandchildren every day are the healthiest of all.","Caring for grandchildren has no measurable effect on mental health.","Grandparents who care for children receive higher pensions."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第2段第3句：those who care for grandchildren several days a week report better mental health than those who do not。B偷换范围，原文是 several days a week 而非每天，也未说是最健康的；C与原文相反；D是末段部分地区政策，并非研究结论。"},{"id":"r08q3","stem":"According to paragraph 3, what is one cost of grandparental childcare?","options":["Children learn fewer social skills than before.","The government has to pay for extra school places.","Parents become less willing to visit their own parents.","Some grandparents give up paid work earlier and save less for old age."],"answer":3,"explain":"答案D。依据在第3段第3句：Some give up paid work earlier than they intended, which reduces their savings for old age，前两句还提到照护体力消耗大、可能忽视自身健康。A、B、C均属无中生有。"},{"id":"r08q4","stem":"What does the author mean by saying that generosity that is expected is another thing?","options":["Grandparents should never refuse to help their children.","Care given out of obligation is different from care given freely.","Parents should pay grandparents for the time they spend.","Families should rely only on public childcare."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。该句前一句说有些人发现自己无法拒绝，因为替代方案是子女付不起的账单，因此被当作理所当然的付出就不再是自愿的慷慨，可推知B。A与作者对无法拒绝的同情相悖；C、D超出了本段论述范围。"},{"id":"r08q5","stem":"The word modest in the last paragraph most probably means","options":["limited in size but useful in purpose","cheap and completely worthless","likely to be cancelled soon","difficult for the public to understand"],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。该句为 Such measures are modest, but they recognise a simple fact，but 表明作者对措施规模有所保留却肯定其意义，故modest指幅度不大。B的 worthless 与but后的肯定矛盾；C、D与语境无关。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"informal","zh":"非正式的"},{"w":"budget","zh":"预算"},{"w":"generosity","zh":"慷慨，大方"},{"w":"neglect","zh":"忽视，忽略"},{"w":"pension","zh":"养老金，退休金"},{"w":"modest","zh":"有限的，不大的"},{"w":"demanding","zh":"费力的，要求高的"},{"w":"rely","zh":"依赖，依靠"}]},{"id":"r09","title":"Why We Buy What We Do Not Need","topic":"消费行为","level":2,"difficulty":"中等","text":"Shops have always tried to persuade, but the techniques available today are far more precise than anything a shopkeeper of the past could imagine. Online stores record what we look at, how long we hesitate and what we put in a basket before abandoning it. The information is then used to decide which advertisement we see next. The goal is not to sell us something we already want; it is to create a want we did not have.\n\nMuch of this works through comparison. A price of 300 yuan looks different when it sits beside an item priced at 900. The expensive product may hardly ever be sold; its real function is to make the cheaper one feel reasonable. Psychologists call this anchoring. The same principle explains why a shop displays a limit of two per customer, a rule that suggests both scarcity and the idea that other people are buying.\n\nSpeed is the other tool. When a decision is made slowly, the reasoning part of the mind has time to ask whether the purchase is really necessary. When it is made in seconds, that question never arrives. One-click ordering, saved card details and delivery within an hour all shorten the distance between a wish and its satisfaction. Convenience is genuine, but it also removes the pause in which people once changed their minds.\n\nUnderstanding these methods does not make anyone immune to them, and shoppers should not be blamed for being influenced. What helps is a small delay built into the system by the buyer rather than the seller: leaving an item in the basket overnight, or asking whether the money would be missed. Retailers study human weakness in detail. Consumers can at least study their own, and often the most useful knowledge is simply the pattern of what they later regret buying. Keeping a short list of those mistakes is more effective than any promise to spend less.","translation":"商店一直在试图说服顾客，但今天可用的手段比过去的店主所能想象的要精确得多。网店会记录我们看了什么、犹豫了多久、把什么放进购物车又放弃。这些信息随后被用来决定我们接下来看到哪条广告。其目的并不是把我们已经想要的东西卖给我们，而是制造出一个我们原本没有的欲望。\n\n其中很大一部分靠比较起作用。300元的价格放在一件标价900元的商品旁边，看上去就不一样了。那件昂贵商品可能几乎卖不出去，它真正的功能是让较便宜的那件显得合理。心理学家称之为锚定效应。同样的原理也解释了为什么商店会写上限购两件，这条规则同时暗示了稀缺和别的人也在买。\n\n速度是另一个工具。当决定慢慢做出时，头脑中负责推理的部分有时间去问这笔购买是否真的必要；而当决定在几秒钟内做出时，这个问题根本来不及出现。一键下单、保存银行卡信息、一小时内送达，都缩短了愿望与满足之间的距离。便利是实实在在的，但它也取消了人们曾经改变主意的那段停顿。\n\n理解这些手法并不能让人免疫，顾客也不该因为受到影响而受责备。真正有帮助的，是由买方而非卖方在设计里加入一点延迟：把商品放在购物车里过一夜，或者问一问这笔钱会不会被惦记。零售商细致地研究人性的弱点，消费者至少可以研究自己的弱点；而往往最有用的知识，就是自己事后后悔买过什么的规律。把那些错误简单记成一张清单，比任何少花钱的决心都更有效。","questions":[{"id":"r09q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why online shopping is generally cheaper than shopping in stores.","How shops use information and psychology to create wants, and how buyers can respond.","The reasons why people regret buying expensive products.","How retailers decide the price of a newly launched product."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。第1段讲网店用数据决定广告，第2段讲比较与锚定效应，第3段讲速度与便利，第4段提出买方可以加入延迟并研究自己的消费规律，故B最全面。A文中未作比较；C、D均只涉及个别细节。"},{"id":"r09q2","stem":"According to paragraph 2, what is the real function of a very expensive product displayed beside a cheaper one?","options":["It is usually the best-selling item in the shop.","It is designed only for wealthy customers.","It makes the cheaper product seem reasonably priced.","It warns customers that prices are going to rise."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在第2段第3句：The expensive product may hardly ever be sold; its real function is to make the cheaper one feel reasonable。A与该句 hardly ever be sold 矛盾；B、D均属无中生有。"},{"id":"r09q3","stem":"According to paragraph 3, what is the effect of fast and convenient ordering?","options":["It removes the pause in which buyers may change their minds.","It reduces the number of items that shops must keep in stock.","It makes delivery more expensive for the seller.","It encourages buyers to compare prices more carefully."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。依据在第3段最后两句：一键下单等方式缩短了愿望与满足之间的距离，末句说便利取消了人们曾经改变主意的那段停顿。B、C、D均属无中生有。"},{"id":"r09q4","stem":"What does the author suggest in the last paragraph?","options":["Shoppers should feel guilty about being influenced by advertising.","Retailers alone are responsible for overspending.","Learning one's own pattern of regret is a practical form of protection.","Online shopping should be limited by law."],"answer":2,"explain":"答案C。依据在末段第3句：often the most useful knowledge is simply the pattern of what they later regret buying，末句又说把错误列成清单比下决心更有效。A与 shoppers should not be blamed 相反；B、D均非作者主张。"},{"id":"r09q5","stem":"What is the author's attitude towards shoppers who are influenced by these techniques?","options":["Critical, blaming them for their weakness.","Sympathetic, treating the influence as normal and offering a practical response.","Amused by the way they waste money.","Indifferent to what they choose to buy."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。末段第1句明确说理解这些手法并不能让人免疫，且 shoppers should not be blamed for being influenced，随后给出把商品放一夜、记录后悔清单等可行建议，可见态度是理解并务实。A与原文相反；C、D与作者提供建议的做法不符。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"persuade","zh":"说服，劝说"},{"w":"precise","zh":"精确的，准确的"},{"w":"hesitate","zh":"犹豫，迟疑"},{"w":"scarcity","zh":"稀缺，短缺"},{"w":"immune","zh":"不受影响的，免疫的"},{"w":"retailer","zh":"零售商"},{"w":"regret","zh":"后悔，懊悔"},{"w":"convenience","zh":"便利，方便"}]},{"id":"r10","title":"Small Habits, Large Effects","topic":"心理与习惯","level":1,"difficulty":"简单","text":"Most people who try to change their behaviour aim too high. They promise to exercise every day, to read a book a week, or to give up sugar completely. Within a month most of these plans have failed. The failure is usually not a lack of willpower. It is a matter of design. A habit that demands a large effort at the beginning is competing with a body and a mind that prefer the way things already are.\n\nResearch on habit formation suggests that the size of the first step matters more than the size of the ambition. A person who decides to walk for five minutes after lunch is far more likely to repeat the action than one who plans an hour at a gym. The five-minute walk is easy to start, easy to repeat and hard to excuse. Repetition, not intensity, is what turns an action into a habit.\n\nThe setting matters too. Habits are triggered by cues: a time of day, a place, or an action that came before. Someone who always reads on the train will find reading difficult at home, where the cue is missing. This is why replacing a habit is often easier than removing one. The old cue remains, but it leads somewhere new. People who want to stop checking their phone at breakfast may do better to put a book in the same place where the phone used to lie.\n\nNone of this means that large goals are worthless. They give direction. But they should be translated into steps small enough to survive a busy week, an illness or a bad mood. Progress that is invisible is still progress, and the person who walks five minutes every day for a year has done something that the person who planned an hour and stopped after a fortnight has not. In the end, consistency beats enthusiasm, because enthusiasm is a visitor and consistency is a resident.","translation":"大多数试图改变自己行为的人都把目标定得太高。他们承诺每天锻炼、每周读一本书，或者彻底戒掉糖。不到一个月，这些计划大多就失败了。失败通常不是因为缺乏意志力，而是设计上的问题。一个一开始就要求付出巨大努力的习惯，是在与一个更偏爱现状的身体和头脑较劲。\n\n关于习惯养成的研究表明，第一步的大小比抱负的大小更重要。决定午饭后走五分钟的人，远比计划去健身房练一小时的人更可能把这件事重复下去。五分钟的散步容易开始、容易重复，也很难找借口不做。把一个行为变成习惯的，是重复，而不是强度。\n\n环境也很重要。习惯由提示触发：一天中的某个时间、某个地点，或之前发生的某个动作。总在火车上读书的人，在家里会觉得读书很难，因为那里缺少那个提示。这就是为什么替换一个习惯往往比消除一个习惯更容易：旧的提示还在，但它通向了新的地方。想改掉早餐时看手机的人，也许把一本书放在手机原来摆放的位置会更有效。\n\n这一切并不是说宏大目标毫无价值，它们提供方向。但宏大目标应当被拆解成足够小的步骤，以便能熬过忙碌的一周、一场病或一次坏心情。看不见的进步仍然是进步；每天走五分钟、坚持一年的人，做到了那个计划练一小时却在两周后放弃的人没能做到的事。归根结底，坚持胜过热情，因为热情是位访客，而坚持是常住居民。","questions":[{"id":"r10q1","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why small first steps are more effective than ambitious plans in forming habits.","Why most people completely lack the willpower to change their behaviour.","How to design a weekly exercise programme for beginners.","The advantages of reading books while travelling by train."],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。第1段指出目标定得太高是失败主因，第2段说明第一步要小，第3段说明提示与环境的作用，第4段强调把小目标拆成小步骤并坚持，故A最全面。B与第1段 The failure is usually not a lack of willpower 相反；C、D只是举例。"},{"id":"r10q2","stem":"According to paragraph 2, why is a five-minute walk after lunch more likely to be repeated?","options":["Because it produces physical results more quickly.","Because it is easy to start, easy to repeat and hard to excuse.","Because it takes place in the morning when energy is higher.","Because it can replace an hour of training at a gym."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。依据在第2段第3句：The five-minute walk is easy to start, easy to repeat and hard to excuse。A、D属无中生有，原文并未说它能替代健身房一小时；C与 after lunch 的时间不符。"},{"id":"r10q3","stem":"According to paragraph 3, what makes replacing a habit easier than removing it?","options":["New habits need less willpower than old ones.","Removing a habit always requires moving to a new house.","Habits are controlled mainly by the time of day.","The old cue can be kept but directed towards a new action."],"answer":3,"explain":"答案D。依据在第3段第4、5句：This is why replacing a habit is often easier than removing one. The old cue remains, but it leads somewhere new，随后用早餐看手机改为放一本书为例。A文中未提；B绝对化；C把提示的种类之一当成了全部。"},{"id":"r10q4","stem":"What does the author imply by saying that progress that is invisible is still progress?","options":["Only progress that other people notice is worth making.","Improvements that are hard to see still count.","People should record their progress every single day.","Large goals should be given up altogether."],"answer":1,"explain":"答案B。该句位于末段，前一句说大目标应拆成能熬过忙碌一周的小步骤，后一句对比每天走五分钟坚持一年的人与计划一小时却两周就放弃的人，可见作者强调看不见的进步仍有价值。A与原文相反；C无中生有；D与末段 large goals give direction 矛盾。"},{"id":"r10q5","stem":"In the last sentence, the author says that consistency is a resident in order to show that","options":["it lasts, unlike enthusiasm, which comes and goes","it stays in one particular house or place","it has to be paid for like rent","it is much rarer than enthusiasm"],"answer":0,"explain":"答案A。该句为 consistency beats enthusiasm, because enthusiasm is a visitor and consistency is a resident，用访客与常住居民的对比说明热情短暂、坚持长久，故A正确。B照字面理解比喻；C、D均非该比喻的含义。"}],"vocab":[{"w":"willpower","zh":"意志力，自控力"},{"w":"ambition","zh":"抱负，目标"},{"w":"repetition","zh":"重复，反复"},{"w":"intensity","zh":"强度，剧烈程度"},{"w":"cue","zh":"提示，触发信号"},{"w":"trigger","zh":"触发，引发"},{"w":"consistency","zh":"一贯性，坚持不懈"},{"w":"fortnight","zh":"两星期，两周"}]}]};
